From: challoc <noreply@chainguard.dev>
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] redisFork: challoc's fork epoch around a snapshot child,
 a new object instead of the value swap while it is open, and the drain
 after it

Redis 8.10.3 (tag 8.10.3, commit 1a2574ce1551d3de6e0b4a56756c0fdbc6659e4f),
on top of 1/6 (MALLOC=challoc) and 2/6, and of 5/6 (no write to an
overwritten or deleted value), without which the epoch saves half as much.
The Redis counterpart of contrib/valkey/0004.

redisFork opens challoc's fork epoch (challoc_cow_begin, <challoc/challoc.h>)
just before forking a mutually exclusive child (RDB, AOF rewrite, full
sync, module fork) and resetChildState closes it (challoc_cow_end) once
the child is reaped, as does the fork's failure path. While the epoch is
open, challoc serves the parent's allocations from pages the child does
not share and hands out no block freed during it that the child may still
share.

Redis needs one change Valkey does not: dbSetValue keeps the old kvobj in
the keyspace and swaps the new value's ptr, type and encoding into it when
neither is an embedded string, which writes the old object's page. Every
64-byte value is such a raw string in Redis (its embstr limit is 44
bytes). While the epoch is open (cow_epoch_open, declared in server.h)
dbSetValue takes its other path, the one embedded strings always take: a
new kvobj with the key, which the epoch places on an unshared page, and
the old one freed.

The blocks written during the epoch then sit above the holes it left.
computeDefragCycles starts a defrag cycle while challoc reports a size
class still draining (challoc_cow_stat(CHALLOC_COW_DRAINING)), at
active-defrag-cycle-max, whatever the fragmentation thresholds and the
decay: challoc's utilisation query tells the defragmenter to move those
blocks back. With activedefrag off nothing moves them, and they return as
the keys are overwritten or deleted.

All of it is compiled only under challoc (CHALLOC_COW, which
<challoc/challoc.h> defines), and the three entry points are weak
references: on a libchalloc.so.0 without fork epochs they are null and
the server runs as without this patch.

Measured in a develop session (8 vCPU, 31 GiB, amd64), 1M (64 B) or 200k
(1 KiB) random keys changed during a 60 s BGSAVE, rdb_last_cow_size in
MiB, challoc with 1/6 to 4/6 against 1/6 to 6/6:

  10M x 64 B SET      1175 -> 235
  10M x 64 B DEL       651 -> 237
  2M x 1 KiB SET       891 ->  30

